WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.850
Welcome to Part 2 of the implementation
series for HR-driven identity.

2
00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.995
Part 1 named one writer for every
account state, brought employees and

3
00:00:08.995 --> 00:00:12.925
contractors in from their sources,
and got joiners ready on day one.

4
00:00:12.925 --> 00:00:15.338
Part 2 covers the last four gates.

5
00:00:15.338 --> 00:00:18.381
First, access assigned from HR attributes,

6
00:00:18.381 --> 00:00:21.338
with a review of what
a move leaves behind.

7
00:00:21.338 --> 00:00:25.275
Then PIV and CAC credentials
tied to HR events.

8
00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:28.675
Then separations, timed
and proven from the logs.

9
00:00:28.675 --> 00:00:33.537
And finally drift, metrics and
the evidence an ISSO files.

10
00:00:33.537 --> 00:00:35.663
The format is the same as Part 1.

11
00:00:35.663 --> 00:00:41.477
Each gate gets its steps, the workbook's
lab commands, the federal requirement

12
00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:47.200
and evidence, availability in GCC and GCC
High, an interim path, and a rollback.

13
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.024
Design guidance is labeled,

14
00:00:49.024 --> 00:00:53.100
and the code is tested against
fixtures, not a live tenant.

15
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.325
Here are Part 2's four gates on one page.

16
00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:04.048
The access gate is done when department
access is assigned automatically, the

17
00:01:04.048 --> 00:01:06.446
supervisor HR names approves requests,

18
00:01:06.446 --> 00:01:09.425
and anything left over
after a move gets reviewed.

19
00:01:09.425 --> 00:01:13.782
The credentials gate is done when
every card issuer is trusted with a

20
00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:18.675
revocation list, and cards are issued
and revoked by HR and contractor events.

21
00:01:18.675 --> 00:01:22.747
The separation gate is done when a
leaver's access is blocked first,

22
00:01:22.747 --> 00:01:25.783
credentials and devices
are revoked in parallel,

23
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:29.263
and the account is deleted
last, with the timing measured.

24
00:01:29.263 --> 00:01:32.956
The drift gate is done when drift
is found and fixed at its source,

25
00:01:32.956 --> 00:01:36.162
and the metrics are reported
on the monitoring cadence.

26
00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:40.862
Each gate has its own rollback, except
the last, which is a standing loop.

27
00:01:40.862 --> 00:01:42.687
Together they finish the track.

28
00:01:42.687 --> 00:01:46.170
Every entitlement traces
back to a rule and a record,

29
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.330
and every separation blocks
in time and leaves evidence.

30
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.968
Dynamic groups carry access that
a rule can decide on its own.

31
00:01:57.968 --> 00:02:02.606
Access packages carry what a rule
can't, with approvals and reviews.

32
00:02:02.606 --> 00:02:05.630
Department packages are
assigned automatically.

33
00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:10.168
A policy gives the package to everyone
who matches a rule on user attributes,

34
00:02:10.168 --> 00:02:12.617
and takes it away when they stop matching.

35
00:02:12.617 --> 00:02:15.942
Each policy covers up to
fifteen thousand users.

36
00:02:15.942 --> 00:02:19.456
Write the rules on the
coded attributes HR owns.

37
00:02:19.456 --> 00:02:22.030
Keep one automatic policy per package.

38
00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:27.080
If a user matches two policies, or moves
between them, they can lose access.

39
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.280
Each policy also creates a hidden
dynamic group, so leave it alone.

40
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.555
One date matters.

41
00:02:32.555 --> 00:02:36.650
From November 3, 2026, any
policy whose rule uses group

42
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.768
membership is quarantined, and it
stops adding or removing anyone.

43
00:02:41.768 --> 00:02:46.092
Microsoft publishes a read-only script
that finds them, so run it now.

44
00:02:46.092 --> 00:02:50.629
Finally, a policy can keep access for
a grace period after someone moves.

45
00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:55.480
Use that for standard access, and give
privileged access no grace period.

46
00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:00.862
Next, decide who approves,

47
00:03:00.862 --> 00:03:04.592
what happens when someone moves,
and where privilege goes.

48
00:03:04.592 --> 00:03:09.667
Requests go to the manager as approver,
with a fallback for anyone without one.

49
00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:13.280
That makes the supervisor HR
names the one who approves.

50
00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.120
When someone changes organization,
the mover workflow removes

51
00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.380
the old department's packages
and requests the new one.

52
00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.730
Then it starts a review of everything
the person holds outside packages.

53
00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.093
That leftover access is the residual,

54
00:03:27.093 --> 00:03:31.743
and the time from transfer to review
is the metric for personnel transfer.

55
00:03:31.743 --> 00:03:34.755
Privileged access stays
out of rules altogether.

56
00:03:34.755 --> 00:03:38.045
HR attributes decide who
may request eligibility,

57
00:03:38.045 --> 00:03:40.943
and the role itself is
activated through PIM.

58
00:03:40.943 --> 00:03:43.918
And treat a reorganization as a mass move.

59
00:03:43.918 --> 00:03:47.073
Rehearse it, let the
execution limit quarantine it,

60
00:03:47.073 --> 00:03:50.740
and release it only after
you've checked it against HR.

61
00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:58.365
The workbook's fourth chapter keeps
the policies as reviewed files.

62
00:03:58.365 --> 00:04:03.301
Run against five broken ones, its lint
catches a rule built on group membership,

63
00:04:03.301 --> 00:04:07.478
and two policies on one package
that the same employees both match.

64
00:04:07.478 --> 00:04:10.931
It also catches a policy
that adds but never removes,

65
00:04:10.931 --> 00:04:15.590
an approval with no fallback, and
privileged access assigned by a rule.

66
00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:18.751
A separate scan reads the
tenant's existing policies

67
00:04:18.751 --> 00:04:21.553
and finds the one still
using group membership.

68
00:04:21.553 --> 00:04:22.815
Then the residual.

69
00:04:22.815 --> 00:04:26.003
A fictional employee moved
departments in October.

70
00:04:26.003 --> 00:04:30.739
For each package they hold, the code checks
the rule against their new attributes.

71
00:04:30.739 --> 00:04:34.953
The old baseline goes after the
grace period, and the new one stays.

72
00:04:34.953 --> 00:04:37.382
Three items go to the manager of record:

73
00:04:37.382 --> 00:04:41.178
one requested package, and two
groups held outside packages.

74
00:04:41.178 --> 00:04:44.324
One finding stands out:
the review is still open

75
00:04:44.324 --> 00:04:48.070
thirteen days after the
move, past the program's ten.

76
00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:56.684
On the federal side, account management,
least privilege and separation of duties

77
00:04:56.684 --> 00:05:01.620
call for the policy exports, with their
rules and the separation-of-duties settings.

78
00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:06.657
Personnel transfer calls for the
transfer-to-review times and the review decisions.

79
00:05:06.657 --> 00:05:11.782
Account review results must be exported
before Entra's thirty-day history closes.

80
00:05:11.782 --> 00:05:17.231
CISA's baseline requires highly privileged
roles to come only through a privileged

81
00:05:17.231 --> 00:05:21.451
access system, so the ISSO shows
PIM eligibility through packages,

82
00:05:21.451 --> 00:05:23.907
and no standing role assigned by a rule.

83
00:05:23.907 --> 00:05:27.821
To validate, compare membership
before and after a test move.

84
00:05:27.821 --> 00:05:30.146
No kit check reads the policies yet,

85
00:05:30.146 --> 00:05:33.595
so keep the export and a
clean group-membership scan.

86
00:05:33.595 --> 00:05:37.420
Most features are available in
Azure Government, with one gap.

87
00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:42.026
Administrative units with dynamic
membership aren't available there,

88
00:05:42.026 --> 00:05:45.695
so GCC High uses assigned units,
filled by a scheduled job.

89
00:05:45.695 --> 00:05:49.266
To roll back, remove the
policy, but keep the old dynamic

90
00:05:49.266 --> 00:05:53.130
group until the package has
run clean for a full cycle.

91
00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.818
A PIV card without a binding to
the account is just a badge.

92
00:06:00.818 --> 00:06:05.367
The binding names one specific
certificate, so any reissue changes it.

93
00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:08.699
The first step is to trust
each issuing authority,

94
00:06:08.699 --> 00:06:11.055
and give each one a revocation list.

95
00:06:11.055 --> 00:06:14.330
Without a list, Entra doesn't
check revocation at all.

96
00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.118
The limits depend on the cloud.

97
00:06:16.118 --> 00:06:20.673
For interactive sign-in, a list
can be twenty megabytes in GCC and

98
00:06:20.673 --> 00:06:25.056
forty-five in GCC High, and it
must download within ten seconds.

99
00:06:25.056 --> 00:06:27.480
If Entra can't download a configured list,

100
00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:31.617
certificate sign-in fails for every
certificate from that authority.

101
00:06:31.617 --> 00:06:36.768
So host the lists on highly available
endpoints, ideally over HTTP.

102
00:06:36.768 --> 00:06:41.648
Next, raise PIV certificates to
multifactor strength by issuer or policy,

103
00:06:41.648 --> 00:06:46.330
and require phishing-resistant
strength in Conditional Access.

104
00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.630
The rest of this gate ties the
card's life to the person's.

105
00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:57.380
No card is requested without
a joiner record and a sponsor.

106
00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.777
The card system writes the
binding when the card is issued,

107
00:07:00.777 --> 00:07:03.042
and removes it when the card is revoked.

108
00:07:03.042 --> 00:07:07.877
Once the binding lands, the temporary pass
from gate three isn't needed anymore,

109
00:07:07.877 --> 00:07:10.704
and the proofing level
is recorded per person.

110
00:07:10.704 --> 00:07:14.655
The card system's events flow
back in, keyed on the person key.

111
00:07:14.655 --> 00:07:18.827
Those events are termination,
revocation, suspension and expiry,

112
00:07:18.827 --> 00:07:21.918
and the same event can
mean two different things.

113
00:07:21.918 --> 00:07:26.592
For a contractor, a terminated or
expired card ends the engagement.

114
00:07:26.592 --> 00:07:30.305
For an employee, it's credential
drift, not a separation,

115
00:07:30.305 --> 00:07:33.605
because only HR decides
when an employee leaves.

116
00:07:33.605 --> 00:07:36.255
And a legal name change
rewrites the binding.

117
00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:39.354
The guide is open that the
card system's interface,

118
00:07:39.354 --> 00:07:42.960
and the contractor PIV path,
are still pending proof.

119
00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.310
The workbook's fifth
chapter does two things.

120
00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.606
First, it measures each
revocation list once,

121
00:07:52.606 --> 00:07:56.347
recording its size, its
download time, and any error.

122
00:07:56.347 --> 00:08:00.510
A failed download is the finding,
so it's recorded, not raised.

123
00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:04.235
Then it checks the tenant's settings
against the limits for a named cloud.

124
00:08:04.235 --> 00:08:06.485
The fictional tenant tells a story.

125
00:08:06.485 --> 00:08:08.896
One certificate authority was renewed,

126
00:08:08.896 --> 00:08:13.060
so the card program's expected
authority is gone from the trust store.

127
00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:16.847
The renewed list is eighteen
and a half million bytes,

128
00:08:16.847 --> 00:08:21.322
takes over eleven seconds to
download, and is served over HTTPS.

129
00:08:21.322 --> 00:08:24.223
The slow download fails in every cloud.

130
00:08:24.223 --> 00:08:27.809
The policy mapping points at
the wrong PIV identifier.

131
00:08:27.809 --> 00:08:31.748
And the phishing-resistant policy
is still in report-only mode.

132
00:08:31.748 --> 00:08:35.734
Run the same check for GCC High
and one warning disappears.

133
00:08:35.734 --> 00:08:40.650
That size is close to the commercial
limit, but well under Azure Government's.

134
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.407
On the federal side, accepting PIV
credentials and public-key authentication call

135
00:08:49.407 --> 00:08:53.978
for the certificate sign-in configuration
export: the trusted authorities,

136
00:08:53.978 --> 00:08:58.187
the revocation settings, the binding
rules, and the required affinity.

137
00:08:58.187 --> 00:09:02.272
CISA's baseline requires
phishing-resistant MFA for all

138
00:09:02.272 --> 00:09:06.526
users and for privileged roles,
so keep the ScubaGear report.

139
00:09:06.526 --> 00:09:11.563
FIPS 201 allows issuance only once
a proper authority authorizes it,

140
00:09:11.563 --> 00:09:16.513
so the card system's records are
joined to the HR or contractor event.

141
00:09:16.513 --> 00:09:21.825
To validate, one kit check lists card
holders with no binding, or only weak ones.

142
00:09:21.825 --> 00:09:24.162
No kit check reads the configuration,

143
00:09:24.162 --> 00:09:28.987
so the evidence includes a revoked test
certificate being refused at sign-in.

144
00:09:28.987 --> 00:09:33.676
Everything here is available in both
clouds, though the size limits differ.

145
00:09:33.676 --> 00:09:36.876
Until there's a clear way
to write the binding after

146
00:09:36.876 --> 00:09:40.000
Active Directory retires,
keep it in AD and sync it.

147
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.336
To roll back, restore the
bindings from the card system,

148
00:09:43.336 --> 00:09:45.630
never by editing the directory.

149
00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:53.393
The separation gate puts the leaver
in the Federal Modernization

150
00:09:53.393 --> 00:09:56.530
Workbook's Book 03's order
and then proves the timing.

151
00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.668
Disable the account and
revoke its sessions first.

152
00:09:59.668 --> 00:10:03.098
Then one custom task starts
the device retire and the

153
00:10:03.098 --> 00:10:06.606
card revocation together,
and waits for both to report.

154
00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:11.285
That wait can run from thirty minutes
to three hours, and if it times out,

155
00:10:11.285 --> 00:10:14.755
it goes to a high-priority
exception, not a retry loop.

156
00:10:14.755 --> 00:10:18.630
Then come the removals, and a
read-back to confirm the block took.

157
00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.942
A disabled account with live
sessions is an incident.

158
00:10:21.942 --> 00:10:26.955
Deletion comes last, in a separate
workflow, after the devices are retired.

159
00:10:26.955 --> 00:10:28.775
Set the leave time late in the day,

160
00:10:28.775 --> 00:10:31.742
and remember the schedule is
part of the time allowed.

161
00:10:31.742 --> 00:10:34.943
An emergency means an
immediate out-of-band block,

162
00:10:34.943 --> 00:10:38.143
then the same sequence on
demand, behind approval.

163
00:10:38.143 --> 00:10:40.722
A separation entered more than three days

164
00:10:40.722 --> 00:10:43.155
late misses the trigger,
so run it by hand.

165
00:10:43.155 --> 00:10:45.810
Privileged leavers need their own path.

166
00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:54.010
The workbook's sixth chapter measures
each separation from the audit logs.

167
00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:56.362
A leaver counts as blocked once both the

168
00:10:56.362 --> 00:10:59.285
disable and the session
revocation are recorded.

169
00:10:59.285 --> 00:11:03.097
They count as fully revoked when the
card and the devices are done too.

170
00:11:03.097 --> 00:11:05.523
Nine fictional separations are measured.

171
00:11:05.523 --> 00:11:08.522
The typical block lands
in about fifteen minutes.

172
00:11:08.522 --> 00:11:11.694
But the slowest ten percent
take over five hours,

173
00:11:11.694 --> 00:11:16.134
against a sample limit of four, and
full revocation takes far longer.

174
00:11:16.134 --> 00:11:17.697
Three stories stand out.

175
00:11:17.697 --> 00:11:21.622
One leaver was blocked late, and
that person's card was revoked

176
00:11:21.622 --> 00:11:25.623
twenty-three hours after notice,
past the eighteen FIPS allows.

177
00:11:25.623 --> 00:11:28.723
Another separation was
entered almost a week late,

178
00:11:28.723 --> 00:11:32.784
and its only disable failed, so that
person was never blocked at all.

179
00:11:32.784 --> 00:11:37.135
And the pipeline re-enabled a third
leaver two days after the disable.

180
00:11:37.135 --> 00:11:39.110
These limits are only samples.

181
00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:43.910
The agency sets the real period, and
sets it together with the schedule.

182
00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:52.405
On the federal side, personnel termination
and account disabling call for both

183
00:11:52.405 --> 00:11:55.928
measured times for each leaver,
along with the workflow run,

184
00:11:55.928 --> 00:12:00.385
the device actions, and the card revocation
with the list that published it.

185
00:12:00.385 --> 00:12:03.560
Contractors add the time
from end date to block.

186
00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:07.539
FIPS 201 says a card that can't
be collected must be revoked

187
00:12:07.539 --> 00:12:11.435
within eighteen hours of
notice, so keep both timestamps.

188
00:12:11.435 --> 00:12:14.422
Emergency disables are logged as changes.

189
00:12:14.422 --> 00:12:18.143
To validate, run a full pay
period with no missed leavers,

190
00:12:18.143 --> 00:12:21.622
blocks within the limit,
and every exception closed.

191
00:12:21.622 --> 00:12:23.486
Security owns this gate.

192
00:12:23.486 --> 00:12:27.623
Lifecycle Workflows and PIM are
available in Azure Government.

193
00:12:27.623 --> 00:12:30.370
The guide infers that a GCC tenant's Logic

194
00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:33.360
App sits in a commercial
Azure subscription.

195
00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:38.772
Until the extensions exist, use a leaver
checklist with a named owner for each row.

196
00:12:38.772 --> 00:12:43.223
To roll back a cancelled separation,
re-enable from the corrected record.

197
00:12:43.223 --> 00:12:46.930
A deleted cloud user can be
restored for thirty days.

198
00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:52.755
The last gate keeps the record true.

199
00:12:52.755 --> 00:12:57.911
Run the lifecycle drift checks: missing
identity, stale access, orphan accounts,

200
00:12:57.911 --> 00:13:02.755
unauthorized writes, and credential
drift for employees and contractors.

201
00:13:02.755 --> 00:13:06.248
Fix each finding at its source,
and let the fix flow back

202
00:13:06.248 --> 00:13:09.742
through the pipeline, never
as a direct directory edit.

203
00:13:09.742 --> 00:13:12.506
Report six metrics on
the monitoring cadence.

204
00:13:12.506 --> 00:13:18.567
Prove HR is the only source, using seven
days of logs, then close the ticket path.

205
00:13:18.567 --> 00:13:23.406
And keep the evidence outside the directory,
because it has to outlive the accounts.

206
00:13:23.406 --> 00:13:25.818
The table shows the workbook's lab run.

207
00:13:25.818 --> 00:13:29.893
Blocking, revocation, joiner
readiness, correlation coverage,

208
00:13:29.893 --> 00:13:34.830
and the share of access granted by rules
are all out of bounds on this fixture.

209
00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:39.067
Transfer-to-review isn't measured,
because a review is still open.

210
00:13:39.067 --> 00:13:43.618
A metric with no input is reported
as not measured, never as zero.

211
00:13:43.618 --> 00:13:46.610
And the thresholds are
for the agency to choose.

212
00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:53.858
The workbook's seventh chapter
makes this gate one run,

213
00:13:53.858 --> 00:13:56.035
plus a chain of evidence packages.

214
00:13:56.035 --> 00:14:01.535
The run reads the earlier labs' outputs,
checking each one against its own manifest.

215
00:14:01.535 --> 00:14:05.151
It insists on knowing whether
it's a fixture or a tenant run,

216
00:14:05.151 --> 00:14:07.185
so a lab can't pass for production.

217
00:14:07.185 --> 00:14:12.097
The evidence tool packages each run with
checksums, chained to the one before.

218
00:14:12.097 --> 00:14:15.036
Then the period report
verifies the whole chain.

219
00:14:15.036 --> 00:14:18.154
Here the chain is unbroken
and nothing is missing,

220
00:14:18.154 --> 00:14:22.110
but twenty findings are still
open, so the period isn't complete.

221
00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:26.710
On the federal side, continuous monitoring
calls for the six metrics per period.

222
00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:29.865
Audit review and retention
call for the log export,

223
00:14:29.865 --> 00:14:34.372
findings closed at the source, and
retention set to the records schedule.

224
00:14:34.372 --> 00:14:38.310
CISA's continuous reporting
calls for ScubaGear results.

225
00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:42.085
The kit checks run on the drift
cadence, and the CISO owns it.

226
00:14:42.085 --> 00:14:46.120
There's no rollback here, because
this is the loop that keeps running.

227
00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:54.133
Put together, the gates make one
evidence file for each population.

228
00:14:54.133 --> 00:14:58.094
It holds the register, the
sources and the reconciliations.

229
00:14:58.094 --> 00:15:03.320
It holds the workflows and their run history,
and the access policies and reviews.

230
00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:06.782
It holds the certificate
configuration and the card records.

231
00:15:06.782 --> 00:15:10.007
It holds the separation
times and their exceptions.

232
00:15:10.007 --> 00:15:14.383
And it holds the drift findings, the
metrics and the sole-source proof.

233
00:15:14.383 --> 00:15:18.357
File every kit run with its
date, tenant and check version.

234
00:15:18.357 --> 00:15:22.854
To build the code for these four
gates, use the developer workbook,

235
00:15:22.854 --> 00:15:27.683
Building HR-Driven Identity, chapters
four to seven, each with its labs.

236
00:15:27.683 --> 00:15:30.320
For the design, see Book 03.

237
00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:32.542
For the attributes these gates read,

238
00:15:32.542 --> 00:15:36.170
see the attribute-driven
migration implementation guide.

239
00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:40.893
To learn it all in order, use the
learning path for HR-driven identity:

240
00:15:40.893 --> 00:15:45.870
seven modules, one per gate, each with
a fixture lab and a knowledge check.

241
00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:48.082
The pipeline makes the account exist.

242
00:15:48.082 --> 00:15:51.624
These seven gates make the HR
record decide what happens to it,

243
00:15:51.624 --> 00:15:53.900
and they leave the evidence that it did.
